		678 -> 689;
	699 [label="Update boolean DrifterLiar",color=orange];
		699 -> 710;
	700 [label="Update boolean LyonSuspicious",color=orange];
		700 -> 710;
	701 [label="I wonder how much our faiths influenced each other, intentionally or unintentionally as the case may be.",shape=box,color=blue];
		701 -> 704;
	698 [label="Fascinating! as a scholar of the cloth, I do hope we have time to learn from one another at length of our respective beliefs.",shape=box,color=blue];
		698 -> 704;
	695 [label="It's a long story. ",shape=box,color=red];
		695 -> 707;
	696 [label="I studied a bunch of different religions, and none of them really suited me. I wound up with sort of a weird collection of humanity.",shape=box,color=red];
		696 -> 708;
	697 [label="I believe what I feel. And I don't need a building or a book to tell me what that is.",shape=box,color=red];
		697 -> 709;
	690 [label="Truly? I am... surprised. Surprised, but pleased. And it is no wonder you are curious, then. Our teachings must be so very foreign to what you know. So much time has passed.",shape=box,color=blue];
		690 -> 704;
	691 [label="I respect that. There is honor in recognizing what we cannot know, in seeing the smallness of the human condition.",shape=box,color=blue];
		691 -> 704;
	692 [label="Believe you me, there is no offence taken. In many cases, you are quite correct.",shape=box,color=blue];
		692 -> 705;
		692 -> 706;
	693 [label="I would admit I am jealous that you do not find cause to fret over what may wait for us beyond the veil. Would that more others did, our world would have known more peace in its days.",shape=box,color=blue];
		693 -> 704;
	694 [label="That is a topic over which I wish to talk with you at great length. Simply not now, at a time when I am not prepared for such a discussion.",shape=box,color=blue];
		694 -> 704;
	688 [label="A wise place to begin. To know a culture by its holidays is to know its heart. For how a people celebrates is how they would be remembered. For those moments are the ones cherished most by those who share them.",shape=box,color=blue];
		688 -> 702;
	689 [label="Of course. Let us begin with that which you may already know: Sol and Lua, the sun and the moon. They are deities who are forever transmutable in their aspects.",shape=box,color=blue];
		689 -> 703;
	710 [label="Hm. Allow me to answer your curiosity. In order to avoid boring you to tears, where would you like to begin?",shape=box,color=blue];
		710 -> 665;
		710 -> 664;
	704 [label="And I am remiss in not staying on topic. I fear I have wandered far from the topic at hand.",shape=box,color=blue];
		704 -> 714;
	707 [label="+20 Chemistry",color=orange];
		707 -> 717;
	708 [label="+20 Chemistry",color=orange];
		708 -> 718;
	709 [label="+20 Chemistry",color=orange];
		709 -> 719;
	705 [label="It doesn't mean I'm not interested in learning, however.",shape=box,color=red];
		705 -> 715;
	706 [label="What about in your case?",shape=box,color=red];
		706 -> 716;
	702 [label="Our main and most common holidays are the Winter and Summer Solstices that mark the shortest and longest days of the year.",shape=box,color=blue];
		702 -> 711;
	703 [label="Appearing in many forms over the centuries to many believers. Sometimes male, sometimes female. But always as Sol, the Sun, and Lua, the Moon.",shape=box,color=blue];
		703 -> 712;
		703 -> 713;
	714 [label="You wished to know about the Luminary Orthodoxy, did you not? In order to avoid boring you to tears, where would you like to begin?",shape=box,color=blue];
		714 -> 665;
		714 -> 664;
	717 [label="Mm. And one best told to a trusted friend. I understand completely. Besides which, you came to me for information, not the other way around.",shape=box,color=blue];
		717 -> 704;
	718 [label="A stained glass window of the soul. Shattered when left apart, but what a beautiful image it paints when assembled.",shape=box,color=blue];
		718 -> 704;
	719 [label="The world would be a better place, if it shared your faith.",shape=box,color=blue];
		719 -> 704;
	715 [label="And for that, I laud your curiosity and scholarly nature. ",shape=box,color=blue];
		715 -> 704;
	716 [label="My case is... complex. But I will not lie and say that it has not been devoid of fear. Though religion has brought a flood of it into my life. It has never removed it.",shape=box,color=blue];
		716 -> 704;
	711 [label="Both holidays are marked by great feasts, though they are delineated by whom you share the feasts with. Where the Winter Solstice is for sharing with family and close friends, the Summer Solstice is for strangers.",shape=box,color=blue];
		711 -> 720;
		711 -> 721;
	712 [label="Oh, that makes so much more sense now.",shape=box,color=red];
		712 -> 722;
	713 [label="[Let him continue.]",shape=box,color=red];
		713 -> 723;
	720 [label="Really? Tell me more about the Summer Solstice.",shape=box,color=red];
		720 -> 724;
	721 [label="Tell me more about the Winter Solstice.",shape=box,color=red];
		721 -> 725;
	722 [label="Yes, some sects can be a bit strict in their choice of iconography, declaring that Sol \"Must\" be one way or \"Must\" be another but the Orthodoxy does not recognize this as fact.",shape=box,color=blue];
		722 -> 726;
	723 [label="Each of the Luminaries holds within them multiple aspects. Each aspect can be seen as a function of the whole. Lua, for example, is the mother, the maiden, the crone.",shape=box,color=blue];
		723 -> 731;
	724 [label="The Summer Solstice is for sharing great bounties and for bringing together strangers who have never met. You would gather in a field, bringing food and wares of your own making, it would not matter what.",shape=box,color=blue];
		724 -> 728;
	725 [label="The Winter Solstice is for bracing against the cold and cherishing those who warm us against the darkest times. You decorate a tree with candles, one light for each of those who have gone before who guide the way as the stars in the sky.",shape=box,color=blue];
		725 -> 729;
	726 [label="I expect that whatever has survived to this far-flung future has wound up being... a bit confused in its documentation of my religion.",shape=box,color=blue];
		726 -> 730;
	731 [label="But Lua can also be He who guides souls along the river of tears to the endless night. Death himself. The harbinger and herald.",shape=box,color=blue];
		731 -> 734;
	728 [label="Candles, or bread, or clothes, or paintings. Or books of poetry. Or wreaths of flowers. Even to come empty handed but \"with glad tidings\" is to be seen as a blessing.",shape=box,color=blue];
		728 -> 732;
	729 [label="And atop it, Sol himself, suffering atop the star tree in the darkness. Dimmed, but not forgotten. For this is the time of Lua. And we celebrate her in this time of shadow.",shape=box,color=blue];
		729 -> 733;
	730 [label="I find myself a living archaeological relic. How depressing. But I digress.",shape=box,color=blue];
		730 -> 723;
	734 [label="And in other sects, Death is an old veiled woman. It differs. But it speaks to the same spirit reaching out to all of us. The silvered hand that wishes to guide us in that last moment.",shape=box,color=blue];
		734 -> 737;
	732 [label="A celebration of life - laughter - music - sharing of all of these things, would commence during that, the longest of Sol's days. ",shape=box,color=blue];
		732 -> 735;
	733 [label="And as we draw in close, and we feast upon a roasted bird and root vegetables and all that does not grow fresh in the frozen earth, we hold those that we love. And we tell stories of those that have gone.",shape=box,color=blue];
		733 -> 736;
	737 [label="The moon that slips over the sun during the eclipse, guiding us through the crossroads in that moment where all is lost.",shape=box,color=blue];
		737 -> 739;
	735 [label="All were welcome to make new friends of strangers, and take home that which they needed.",shape=box,color=blue];
		735 -> 738;
	736 [label="We remember, on Lua's longest night, that she will guide us and our family through in the end.",shape=box,color=blue];
		736 -> 738;
	739 [label="Update boolean DrifterEclipse",color=orange];
		739 -> 741;
	738 [label="But the most sacred of holidays to the Luminaries is The Summum Bonum. It is a rare event, when Sol and Lua align into a perfect eclipse.",shape=box,color=blue];
		738 -> 740;
	741 [label="Check boolean VelimirEclipse",color=orange];
		741 -> 744 [label=true,color=green];
		741 -> 743 [label=false,color=brown];
	740 [label="It is a wonderous and holy occasion, where all of nature responds. Have you had such luck as to witness it yourself?",shape=box,color=blue];
		740 -> 742;
	744 [label="Wait...",shape=box,color=red];
		744 -> 747;
	743 [label="Have you ever seen an eclipse yourself?",shape=box,color=red];
		743 -> 746;
	742 [label="Update boolean DrifterEclipse",color=orange];
		742 -> 745;
	747 [label="That sounds... really familiar.",shape=box,color=red];
		747 -> 755;
	746 [label="I have been blessed to witness it twice. And both times, I found it... transformative. If I could stay within that brief moment, suspended there forever, I would.",shape=box,color=blue];
		746 -> 754;
